---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigWorkedExample from '../figures/FigWorkedExample.astro';

const terms = [
  ['c<sub>crypto</sub>(v)', 'Posture', 'Posture of the algorithm reached: 0 post-quantum, 0.3 hybrid, 1 classical.'],
  ['c<sub>alg</sub>(v)', 'Friction', 'Octets of signature and public key the verifier must carry, normalised.'],
  ['c<sub>key</sub>(v)', 'Key strength', 'Strength of the key material, by NIST security category.'],
  ['c<sub>dist</sub>(u, v)', 'Distance', '1 when the hop crosses an administrative boundary, 0 otherwise.'],
];
---

<Chapter id="model" num="05" kicker="The model" title="One governed graph, read by each receiver.">
  <div class="article">
    <p class="intro">
      The federated ecosystem is modelled as a directed graph that changes over time and carries
      its own governance, read from the certificates themselves:
    </p>

    <div class="eq" role="math" aria-label="G at t equals V, E at t, w, Phi">
      <span class="eq__body">G|<sub>t</sub> = (V, E|<sub>t</sub>, w, Φ)</span>
      <span class="eq__n">(1)</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      The vertices <span class="m">V</span> are the certification authorities of the federation,
      with <strong>one node per algorithm</strong>: rotating a key adds a version of a node,
      changing algorithm adds a new node. The edges <span class="m">E|<sub>t</sub></span> are
      derived from real certificates that are valid and not revoked at the instant <span class="m"
        >t</span
      >, never from declarations. They include the cross-certificates between intermediate CAs of
      different domains and the internal edges of each hierarchy.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each edge carries the governance constraints of its certificate, read from <code
        >pathLenConstraint</code
      >, <code>nameConstraints</code> and the policy extensions, and composed along the path with
      the semantics of RFC 5280, section 6:
    </p>

    <div class="eq" role="math" aria-label="Phi of e equals depth, name, pol">
      <span class="eq__body">Φ(e) = ⟨ depth(e), name(e), pol(e) ⟩</span>
      <span class="eq__n">(2)</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      The graph is never read in the abstract. It is always queried by a receiving domain <span
        class="m">d</span
      >, through its policy:
    </p>

    <div class="eq" role="math" aria-label="pi d">
      <span class="eq__body"
        >π<sub>d</sub> = ( TP<sub>d</sub>, s<sub>0</sub>, α, β, γ, δ, τ<sub>d</sub>, σ<sub>d</sub>
        )</span
      >
      <span class="eq__n">(3)</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      that is, the anchors it accepts, the initial validation state, four weights that add up to
      one, a <strong>cryptographic threshold τ<sub>d</sub></strong> and the purposes σ<sub>d</sub>
      the path must allow. A path is <em>admissible</em> for <span class="m">d</span> when it starts
      at one of its anchors, satisfies Φ, keeps every hop under the threshold and allows the
      required purposes:
    </p>

    <div class="eq eq--wide" role="math" aria-label="Admissible paths">
      <span class="eq__body"
        >P<sub>adm</sub>(d, v, t) = &#123; P : tp ⇝ v | tp ∈ TP<sub>d</sub>, P ⊆ E|<sub>t</sub>, P ⊨ Φ,<br />
        ∀e ∈ P : c<sub>crypto</sub>(e) ≤ τ<sub>d</sub>, σ<sub>d</sub> ⊆ ∩ eku(e) &#125;</span
      >
      <span class="eq__n">(4)</span>
    </div>

    <p>Among the admissible paths, each hop has a cost in the receiver's own weights,</p>

    <div class="eq" role="math" aria-label="w d of u v">
      <span class="eq__body"
        >w<sub>d</sub>(u, v) = α c<sub>crypto</sub>(v) + β c<sub>alg</sub>(v) + γ c<sub>key</sub>(v)
        + δ c<sub>dist</sub>(u, v)</span
      >
      <span class="eq__n">(5)</span>
    </div>

    <table class="terms">
      <caption class="label">Terms of the cost, each in [0, 1]</caption>
      <tbody>
        {
          terms.map(([sym, name, text]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <span class="m" set:html={sym} />
              </th>
              <td>
                <span class="terms__name">{name}.</span> {text}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>and the receiver selects the path of least total cost:</p>

    <div class="eq" role="math" aria-label="Selected path">
      <span class="eq__body"
        >P*<sub>d</sub>(v) = Sel( arg min<sub>P ∈ P<sub>adm</sub></sub> Σ<sub>e ∈ P</sub> w<sub
          >d</sub
        >(e) )</span
      >
      <span class="eq__n">(6)</span>
    </div>

    <p>
      where Sel breaks ties deterministically: the fewest certificates first, then the
      lexicographic order of their issuer and serial number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="key">
    <span class="m">Φ</span> and <span class="m">τ<sub>d</sub></span> decide which paths may exist.
    <span class="m">w<sub>d</sub></span> decides which one is preferred.
  </p>

  <div class="article">
    <p>
      Admission is binary and comes first; preference is continuous and only orders what was
      admitted. Two receivers can order the same routes differently, and each is right under its
      own policy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="example-h">A worked example</h3>
  <div class="article">
    <p>
      A receiver in domain B, hybrid with τ<sub>B</sub> = 0.3, validates a certificate issued in
      domain C. Four candidate paths exist. The threshold prunes the one through a classical
      intermediate of A; the depth budget prunes the one through the bridge, inside domain C. Of
      the two survivors, the cost selects the path through the ML-DSA-87 intermediate.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={5} caption="Four candidate paths, two pruned by different constraints, one selected by cost. With a policy that favours bandwidth instead, the hybrid path wins: the crossover can be computed, at β = 0.473.">
    <FigWorkedExample />
  </Figure>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .article .intro {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .article p {
    margin: 0 0 1em;
  }

  .m {
    font-family: var(--font-math);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .eq {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.3rem 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 0 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .eq__body {
    flex: 1;
    font-family: var(--font-math);
    font-size: 1.12rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text-strong);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .eq__body sub {
    font-size: 0.68em;
  }

  .eq__n {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .terms {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0.4rem 0 1.4rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  .terms caption {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    text-align: left;
  }

  .terms th,
  .terms td {
    padding: 0.55rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: baseline;
  }

  .terms th {
    width: 8.5rem;
    padding-right: 1rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .terms__name {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .key sub {
    font-size: 0.65em;
  }

  .key .m {
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .chapter .example-h {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .eq {
      padding-left: 0;
      gap: 0.6rem;
    }
    .eq__body {
      font-size: 1rem;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .terms th {
      width: 6.2rem;
    }
  }
</style>
